(* ============================================================================
   PART 0: LAYOUT LAYER (L0) -- LINE AUTOMATON (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   The block productions (PARTS 1-2) are context-free only OVER THE OUTPUT OF
   THIS LAYER. Indentation, container prefixes, and lazy continuation are 2D
   properties no string grammar can express (they need column arithmetic and
   carried container state); L0 states them as a deterministic one-pass line
   automaton instead of prose. The three engine implementations realize
   this automaton literally.

   INPUT.  Lines, split on `newline` as that production defines it below.

           A LEADING BYTE ORDER MARK IS STRIPPED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-001]. A single U+FEFF
           at the very start of the document is removed before the first line
           is read, so `<BOM># T` is a heading and not paragraph text. ONE, and
           only there: a U+FEFF anywhere else is an ordinary zero-width
           character, and zero-width characters (U+200B, U+FEFF) are NOT
           whitespace and ARE ordinary destination characters.

           A NULL IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-002].
           Every U+0000 in the source becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER,
           wherever it stands, before the first line is read. It is therefore
           not content, and it is the ONE C0 control PART 9 §29 does not make
           content; §29 carries that carve-out. EVERY occurrence is replaced,
           one U+0000 to one U+FFFD, so the transform does not change source
           offsets.

           Column arithmetic is PART 9 §24 C1. A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE
           LEADING RUN (PART 7).
   STATE.  open := stack of open containers, bottom-up (document ->
           innermost), plus the current leaf (none | paragraph | heading |
           ...). Container kinds and the per-line prefix each demands:
             block quote    marker `>` [end of line | one space]
             list item      indentation to content_column (PART 9 §24 C3)
             footnote def   indentation >= 2 spaces; the prefix CONSUMED is
                            exactly 2, content_column := 2 (PART 9 §16)
             fenced body    (code / raw / comment / admonition / div /
                            line block / hard-break block) -- no per-line
                            prefix; bounded by its where-guarded fence pair

           EVERY FRAME CARRIES OWNERSHIP AND PARAGRAPH STATE SEPARATELY --
           NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-003]. A frame is the record

             owner              document | quote | list_item |
                                definition_body | footnote_body | fenced_body
             base_column        authored column at which this frame opened
             content_column     minimum column of ordinary child blocks
             container_open     whether the frame may own another line
             paragraph_open     whether its current leaf accepts lazy text
             last_boundary_kind ordinary | blank | line_comment |
                                fenced_comment | definition | heading | quote |
                                code_fence | raw_fence | colon_fence | table |
                                nested_list | continuation | end

           `container_open` is NEVER inferred from `paragraph_open`.
           `paragraph_open` describes an operation available inside a frame
           that has already survived; it does not keep that frame alive. S1
           and the transition table below select the surviving frames and the
           owner first. Only then may S3/S4 continue a paragraph.

           OWNERSHIP TRANSITIONS. `C` means the current container frame stays
           open after the boundary; `P` independently says whether a paragraph
           is open. `deepest P` means the last child container is followed to
           its deepest leaf. These transitions apply to list items, quotes,
           definition bodies and footnote bodies alike; a fenced body adds the
           opacity rule in S2.

             preceding boundary       container_open   paragraph_open
             ordinary line             C                yes
             blank line                C                no
             line comment              C                no
             fenced comment            C                no
             invisible definition      C                no, for full extent
             heading                   C                no
             block quote               C                deepest P
             closed code/raw fence     C                no
             closed colon fence        C                no
             table                     C                no
             nested list               C                deepest P
             continuation marker       C                deepest attached P
             end of input              no               no

           A closed block therefore has ONE effect in every containing frame:
           it leaves the frame open and leaves no paragraph open. A following
           line may close the frame because its column is owned by an ancestor;
           the closed block itself never closes its owner.

           ONE AUTHORED BLOCK BASE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-004]. Every open
           container which accepts child blocks has a minimum
           `content_column`. A recognized block opener AT that column opens
           there. A recognized opener PAST it establishes its authored visual
           column as the local `base_column` for that ONE complete block; the
           opener, payload, structural continuations, metadata and closer are
           measured from that base. Canonical output returns the block to the
           container's minimum column. Ordinary over-indented text establishes
           no base.

           OWNERSHIP PRECEDES REBASING. The base belongs to the INNERMOST open
           container whose content column the opener reaches. While that block
           is open, a nested body's opener at its own content column is owned
           by that nested body; it MUST NOT be reinterpreted as a fresh sibling
           base in an ancestor. A blank line ends a paragraph, not the authored
           block which contains it. List items, definition bodies, footnote
           bodies and blocks attached by a continuation marker apply this same
           rule; their construct clauses only define the minimum column and
           attachment boundary.

           A BLOCK'S EXTENT ENDS AT A DEFINITION IT CAN ONLY TAKE AS TEXT
           (carve#1918). "At its own content column" is the whole of what the
           nested body owns: ownership is decided per line, so a block standing
           open does not withhold the columns BELOW its own. A definition it
           does not itself register is a line its body never reached, and §10
           I5 has already spent the lazy fold that would be the only other way
           in -- at a container's content column a definition INTERRUPTS the
           open paragraph rather than folding into it. So the extent ends above
           that line, and the line establishes its base in the container whose
           content column it does reach.

           OWNER SELECTION FOR THE NEXT VISIBLE LINE. Apply prefix matching
           first. A quote requires its marker or an eligible stored continuation
           claim; indentation alone never supplies a quote prefix. For an
           indentation container, determine ownership before applying paragraph
           interruption or lazy folding. Opener classification is consulted
           during ownership selection to decide claim eligibility.

           LIST-ITEM PRECEDENCE. PART 9 §24 C3 and CARVE-P9-051/053 qualify
           the table below. After a blank, a line below the item's content
           column belongs outside that item, including the band above its base.
           A heading, table or thematic break on the marker line also leaves
           no ordinary continuation claim. A following line below the content
           column closes that item, including this band (CARVE-P0-007).
           After a line comment consumed below the content column, a noninterrupting
           follower remains in the item even below that column. A comment at
           or above the content column does not grant that retention (C3).
           Sibling markers are recognized before ordinary-line retention.
           A retained list marker below the item's content column remains
           paragraph text. Closing the preceding paragraph does not reduce
           the indentation required for a child list.
           This is retained item ownership, not a stored ordinary-line continuation
           claim. The retention describes the most recent boundary, not the
           next visible line across arbitrary invisible boundaries. A comment
           at or above the content column clears earlier below-column retention.
           A below-column comment is consumed by the item only while an ordinary
           continuation claim or existing below-column retention admits it.
           Without either, the missing prefix closes the item before that comment
           is classified in the enclosing context. A following blank also clears
           retention. An interrupting
           opener below the content column is classified outside the item.
           A matched comment fence at the enclosing context's opener column
           ends the item as specified by C3 and corpus 214; an unmatched fence
           is a line comment and follows the line-comment rule.

             column band                         selected owner
             past base_column but below          this surviving frame; the
               content_column                    residue is classified at its
                                                 local column zero
             at or below base_column             nearest surviving ancestor
             at content_column                   this frame
             past content_column                 this frame, with the authored
                                                 block base of §24 C3 when the
                                                 residue is a recognized opener

           A further qualification is a continuation claim already established
           by the PRECEDING ordinary line: an unblank-separated, noninterrupting
           line that misses a container prefix is assigned to the owner of that
           ordinary line. This is stored ownership, not a search for any open
           paragraph. After the owner is selected, `paragraph_open` decides
           whether the operation is continuation or a new paragraph. A blank,
           heading, definition, comment, table or closed fence establishes no
           such claim.

           Definitions register only in the context selected by this table.
           In particular, a definition below one body's content column does
           not register in that body; column zero belongs to the document or
           another surviving ancestor and registers there.

           COMMENTS ARE CLASSIFIED BEFORE BLOCK OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-005].
           For each line, first honor any active code, raw or
           other opaque payload. Decode the active container prefix and
           authored indentation once. Then recognize `%%` and a matched `%%%`
           span as layout-transparent comment tokens BEFORE selecting an owner
           for visible block classification. Record the comment node, its
           authored spelling and its complete source extent, but remove it from
           visible classification and rendering. This is lexical erasure from
           layout, NEVER erasure from the AST or source map.

           For a comment owned inside a surviving container, both forms apply
           the `line_comment` / `fenced_comment` row above: the container remains
           open and the current paragraph closes. This transition does not put
           an enclosing-context comment fence inside an item it already ended.
           A following blank repeats the blank transition;
           it does not close the container merely because a comment preceded
           it. The next VISIBLE line follows prefix matching, the owner table and
           the list-item qualifications above. A comment creates no stored
           ordinary-line continuation claim. The retained list ownership in
           CARVE-P9-053 is a separate rule, not parser fallthrough.

           A matched `%%%` token carries its opener, body and exact-width
           closer as one source extent across authored indentation. Its body is
           opaque. An opener without an exact-width closer is one `%%` line
           comment; later lines are classified normally. Comment delimiters
           inside code, raw content or another opaque payload are literal
           bytes. Nested containers, quotes, definition bodies and footnote
           bodies use this same lexical phase ordering. Container survival
           remains subject to the prefix and construct-specific ownership rules.
   STEP (per line L, executed exactly once -- no backtracking, Design
         Principle 1: the contribution a line makes to block structure never
         depends on a future line, the only lookahead being the DECLARED
         &(...) closer guards on fence / frontmatter openers):
     S1 MATCH PREFIXES. Walk `open` bottom-up, consuming each container's
        prefix from L (strip `>` or `> ` for quotes; DEDENT by columns for
        indentation containers, PART 9 §24 C5). Stop at the first container
        whose prefix L does not supply.
     S2 FENCED BODY. If the innermost matched container is a fenced body, L
        is verbatim content unless it matches that fence's where-guarded
        closer (then pop). No other rule applies to L.
     S3 FULL MATCH (every prefix consumed). The residue of L continues the
        innermost container: BLANK -> close the open paragraph (recorded for
        tight/loose, PART 9 §17 L1); a BLOCK OPENER -> the interruption
        relation decides (PART 9 §10); a LIST MARKER reaching
        content_column -> sublist (PART 9 §24 C3); otherwise paragraph /
        lazy item text.
     S4 PARTIAL MATCH. The unmatched containers are candidates to close.
        OWNERSHIP FIRST: apply the stored continuation claim and the owner
        table above. If the line is owned by a surviving unmatched frame,
        NOTHING closes. Otherwise close unmatched frames through the selected
        owner and re-classify the residue there (S3). This decision reads
        `container_open`, `last_boundary_kind`, and columns; it does not read
        `paragraph_open`.

        LAZY CONTINUATION SECOND: only after ownership is fixed, if the
        selected frame holds an OPEN PARAGRAPH and the residue is NOT an
        interrupting line (PART 9 §10 -- list markers and plain text fold;
        visible openers interrupt), L folds into that paragraph. With no open
        paragraph, the same already-owned line begins a new paragraph in that
        frame. Lazy continuation therefore changes the leaf operation, never
        which container survives.

        NO STORED CONTINUATION CLAIM, NO LAZY LINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-006].
        With no stored ordinary-line continuation claim, apply the owner table
        and its construct-specific qualifications, even when the item's last
        block is a container. For `. >` / `X`, the table selects the document:
        the item closes and `X` starts a document paragraph because the empty
        quote stored no claim. `- > q` / `lazy` folds
        because the ordinary quoted line stored a claim and its leaf is an
        open paragraph. Select the owner before continuing the leaf.

        THE MARKER LINE'S CONTENT IS THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-007].
        Classify marker-line content as the item's first block, using S4's
        test: does any container in the open stack hold an open paragraph?
        Headings, tables, thematic breaks, comments, link/footnote definitions
        and floating attributes leave none open. Ordinary prose, or a container
        ending in an open paragraph, permits a lazy fold. This applies equally
        to marker-line content, indented blocks and quote bodies.

        A terminated comment span on a nested item's marker line is that
        item's first block. In `- a` / `  - %%%` / `    hidden` / `%%%` /
        `tail`, `tail` is a document paragraph at every closer column. An
        ancestor MUST NOT treat the closer as a fresh below-column comment
        granting retention. An ordinary payload line below the inner item's
        content column still ends that item under S1/S4. Definitions are
        collected where written (SS17 L6); item closure governs the next line
        and MUST NOT discard the definition.

        A FLOATING ATTRIBUTE BLOCK DOES NOT CHANGE THE CONTENT COLUMN --
        NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-008]. Marker-attached attributes contribute zero
        width to `content_column` (§24 C3). A task checkbox is content, not
        marker width. Thus `-{#item} {#next}` / `  # H` keeps the item open and
        attaches `{#next}` to the heading at column 2. An ordered `1.` marker
        places the equivalent heading at column 3.

        Moving the heading one column left closes the item: the floating
        attribute opened no paragraph. Reclassify the residue as document
        paragraph text and discard the item's pending attribute (§15 A4).
        It MUST NOT escape to that paragraph. A task item retains its checkbox
        even when no block remains. Ordinary marker-line prose, which opens a
        paragraph, permits a below-column lazy fold.

        A sibling marker also closes the item and discards pending attributes.
        In `- a` / `  -{#p} b` / `    {#x}` / `  - c`, `{#x}` belongs to the
        first sub-item and is dropped when `- c` closes it. The sublist remains
        one list. For quote content, inspect the last block recursively:
        `- > q` / `tail` folds; `- > # H` / `tail` does not.

        A QUOTE'S LAST BLOCK MAY ITSELF BE A QUOTE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-009].
        Follow the last child container to its leaf at every depth. An outer
        quote ending in an inner heading, table or thematic break has no open
        paragraph, so a following unmarked line cannot lazily continue it.
        A heading at an item's content column likewise leaves no paragraph
        open: a following below-column line cannot stay in that item. Apply
        the owner table to surviving frames. In corpus
        75-list-nesting-and-looseness-4, the inner item closes and `lazy` is
        reclassified in the outer item, whose paragraph is the selected owner.

        A wrapped attribute block is one interrupting block across its opener
        and continuation lines. `- {.a` / `  .b}` / `tail` leaves no paragraph
        open, and `tail` is reclassified outside the item.

        A DEFINITION AT THE CONTENT COLUMN ENDS THE PARAGRAPH, NOT THE
        CONTAINER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-010]. A link or footnote definition
        at a list item's or definition body's `content_column` interrupts the
        paragraph and registers (§10 I5). A comment there also interrupts.
        The container stays open until a later line fails ownership selection.
        Thus `- a` / `  [r]: /u` / `tail` registers `r`, then ends the item and
        publishes `tail` at document level.

        At document column 0 a definition is a document-level interrupter.
        At a nonzero column below `content_column` it is lazy paragraph text
        and does not register: `- a` / ` [r]: /u` / `tail` folds. Abbreviation
        definitions are recognized only at document level (§16); inside a
        container their lines are ordinary text and reopen the paragraph.

        A QUOTE IS REACHED BY ITS MARKER, AND A COLUMN NEVER REACHES INTO ONE.
        Prefix matching requires `>` (§24 C5); indentation cannot supply it.
        An unmarked definition line reaching it through lazy continuation is
        paragraph text and registers nothing. With the marker supplied,
        `> - x` / `>   [r]: /u` registers at the inner item's content column.

        A NEW MARKER DOES NOT REACH A DEAD CONTAINER'S COLUMN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-019].
        A blank ends a block quote and the containers opened inside it. A later
        quote marker opens a new quote without inheriting their columns.

            > - x

            >   [r]: /u

            > [r][]

        No item is open when the definition is read, so its extra indentation
        makes it paragraph text and `[r][]` remains literal. Without the blank
        after `> - x`, the definition registers in the open item.

        With no item open, definitions register at the context's content
        column exactly, or at document column 0. Extra indentation provides
        no registration site. With an item open, §24 C3 accepts at or past its
        content column by erasing the authored block base.

        AT OR PAST MEANS THE DEEPEST COLUMN THE LINE REACHES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-020].
        Register a definition in the innermost open container whose content
        column it reaches. A deeper container that the line does not reach
        neither owns nor withholds it. In `- - x` / `   [r]: /url`, the items'
        content columns are 2 and 4; the definition at column 3 registers in
        the outer item. Columns 2, 4 and 5 also register. Past a content column,
        extra indentation may change the owner but never remove registration.
        Corpus 441 pins the four columns.

        BELOW EVERY OPEN COLUMN IS UNCHANGED. A nonzero-column definition
        below every open content column remains lazy paragraph text and does
        not register (§24 C3).

        A column cannot supply a quote prefix (CARVE-P0-010), but an eligible
        continuation claim keeps the quote open. S4 selects that surviving
        quote before classifying the residue. An unmarked list marker under
        its open paragraph therefore folds under §10 I2, even at an enclosing
        item's content column:

            - a
              > - x
              - m

        This also holds for a quote on the item marker line and at every host
        or quote depth. Quoted prose in place of the quoted list folds alike.
        With an item, heading, fence or paragraph instead of the quote, S4
        selects the enclosing item and §24 C3 opens a list element at its
        content column. A blank ends the quote and its nested columns
        (CARVE-P0-019). Intervening lazy prose keeps the quote open, including
        for following markers (CARVE-P0-015). Corpus 448 pins folds and controls.

        A BLOCK OPENER IN THE BAND FOLDS ON THE SAME TERMS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-021].
        Under a quote holding an open paragraph, a code/raw fence, heading,
        thematic break or table row opens a block only at the host's content
        column. At every other column it folds into the paragraph. For
        `- a` / `  > q`, column 2 opens; column 1 and columns 3 through 8 fold,
        including the quote's column 4. Corpus 531 pins this band in all hosts.

        §2's rebasing is what the band turns on. "A recognized opener PAST that
        column establishes its authored visual column" presumes the line IS a
        recognized opener there, and past the column it is one only where
        nothing can take it as text -- with a container in the open stack
        holding an OPEN PARAGRAPH the line is ordinary over-indented text, and
        §2's last sentence gives such text no base. AT the content column it is
        an opener whatever is open, because that column IS the body's column 0
        (§24 C3) and a block there ends the paragraph it sits under
        (CARVE-P0-011). So `- a` over `  > q` opens a block at column 2 alone.
        Column 1 is below the item's column, columns 3 through 8 are above it,
        column 4 is the quote's own content column, and all of them fold: a line
        that writes no `>` is in no quote at any column, and none of these
        reaches an opener column.

        A blank closes the paragraph. In the host, the same opener then opens
        a sibling block on its authored base (carve#2598).

        At document level an indented fence is text at every nonzero column,
        with or without a blank; no container minimum permits rebasing there.
        Apply the host rule equally to list items, definition bodies and
        footnote bodies. In `:: t` / `:  - a` / `    [r]: /url`, the body's
        content column is 3 and the item's is 5; the definition at column 4
        registers in the body (CARVE-P0-020). A newly marked quote after a
        blank does not revive an ended item (CARVE-P0-019).

        Ordinary prose reopens a paragraph anywhere among a container's
        blocks, including after a table, fence or quote. Following lazy lines
        then continue that paragraph. A blank closes it; with no open paragraph
        to continue, a following below-column line leaves the item whether its
        last block is a terminated or unterminated colon div, an opaque body,
        a quote, or ordinary prose. A line at the item's content column remains
        inside it; under a div it is the div's second block. A missing closer
        does not alter these decisions.

        Compute columns by composing each container's prefix strip (§24 C5)
        and classify in the innermost container's resulting coordinates.
        Host kind, prefix order and nesting depth do not alter that rule.
        A definition either registers or remains visible text; it MUST NOT
        disappear from both rendering and definition tables.

        AN OPAQUE MARKER-LINE QUOTE PRESERVES THE BAND AS TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-022].
        When a code or raw fence opens inside the quote prefix on a list item's
        marker line, an over-indented follower establishes no fresh authored
        base while that fence is open or remains the quote's last block. A later
        open code or raw fence in that same quote also preserves the band; closing that later fence
        restores the authored-base behavior under [CARVE-P0-004]. This applies
        at any quote depth. A quote marker in the band remains literal item
        text; it supplies no prefix to the preceding quote. A line at the item's
        content column still opens there. A blank permits a new authored base.
        A later quoted paragraph ends the fence-tail condition. A heading,
        table or thematic break quote head retains authored-base behavior.
        Comment fences remain governed by PART 9 §28.

        AT A CONTAINER'S CONTENT COLUMN, A BLOCK ENDS THE PARAGRAPH IT SITS
        UNDER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-011]. A block at the content column is
        recognized as at document column 0 (§24 C3). It ends the preceding
        paragraph and leaves the container open, whether the block is visible
        or invisible.
        Comments, link/footnote definitions and attribute blocks all apply.
        A following document-column line leaves the container if no open
        paragraph remains anywhere in the stack.

        This transition covers the block's entire extent. A footnote
        definition's indented continuation belongs to that definition and
        permits no lazy continuation into it. A link reference definition is
        one line; following indented prose is ordinary container text.

        A HEADING IS THE VISIBLE CONTROL -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-012]. A heading
        at the content column leaves no paragraph open. A following line at
        document column 0 ends the item under S4, as after a marker-line
        heading or a heading inside a quote. A line at the item's content column
        remains its second paragraph. At a nonzero column below it, access is
        through S4's lazy fold; §24 C3's comment exception keeps that path open.

        A footnote definition's extent includes blanks between its blocks:
        `- a` / `  [^f]: t` / blank / `    more` / `tail` ends the item and
        gives the note two blocks. Those blanks are interior to the definition.
        A blank after a one-line link definition is outside that definition;
        following indented prose opens the container's second paragraph, and a
        flush-left line folds into it. Nested definition extent is not decided
        by this clause.

        A FENCED BODY IS NOT A PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-013]. If a line
        supplies no indentation prefix to the fence's host, S1 stops there and
        S2 does not apply. A verbatim body holds no open paragraph for S4 to
        continue. In

            - ```
            x
            ```

        the item closes with an empty code block; `x` is document text and the
        trailing run is ordinary inline text.

        Where no container in the stack holds an open paragraph, select the
        nearest surviving ancestor whose content column the line reaches and
        close intervening containers at every depth. The inner item keeps its
        fence body; reclassify the run in the selected owner at its own column
        (carve#2490). With no open paragraph at document level, a run at
        document column 0 opens a code block. With inner
        content column 4, a run at column 0 or 1 reaches the document; a run at
        column 2 reaches an outer item.

        AND NO HOST TERM (carve#2538). This holds for items, definition bodies
        and footnote bodies inside quotes too: an unmarked column-0 line
        reaches the document. Matched `%%%` spans are the exception (PART 9 §28):
        their whitespace slot admits a closer at any column, ending the span
        without ending a container.

        FENCE KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-014].
        Container reach depends on whether an open paragraph exists in the
        stack. Code bodies cannot hold one; colon bodies can. A column-0 line
        ends the host when no paragraph is open; with one open, it takes S4's
        lazy branch.

        A code fence interrupts an open paragraph only when a closer exists
        (§10 I4). Otherwise its line is paragraph text, including at a
        container's content column.

        THE CLOSER LOOKAHEAD DOES NOT STOP AT A BELOW-COLUMN LINE. Search past
        such lines when deciding whether the fence has a closer.
        A BELOW-COLUMN LINE IS SEARCHED PAST, NEVER MATCHED. Count only closers
        inside the container. In

            - a
              ```
              b
             y
              ```

        the indented closer makes a real code block, which ends the item at
        ` y`. Moving the closer to document column 0 makes it ineligible;
        no block opens, and ` y` and the run fold as paragraph text. A blank
        closes the paragraph; a terminated fence leaves no paragraph open.

        A LAZY FOLD KEEPS THE CONTAINER OPEN FOR FOLLOWING LINES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-015].
        A lazy fold closes no container. A later line returning to the
        container's content column remains inside it:

            - x
              :::
              a
            d
              b
              :::

        `a`, `d` and `b` form one paragraph in one div. With no open paragraph,
        the flush-left line ends the container and later lines are outside it.

        A REAL DIV IS A CONTAINER LIKE ANY OTHER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-016].
        An unterminated colon div permits a lazy fold when its last block is
        an open paragraph: `- item` / `  ::: note` / `  body` / `tail` folds.
        Omitting `body` leaves an empty div and no paragraph to continue, so
        `tail` is top-level. Closing the div also closes its paragraph and
        leaves `tail` at top level.

        CONTAINER KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-017].
        Apply the same test in quotes. `> quote` / `> ::: note` / `tail` ends
        the quote, as does the form with `> body` / `> :::` before `tail`.
        An empty or closed colon container leaves no open paragraph.

        DEFINITION BODIES FOLLOW THE SAME CONTAINER REACH RULE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P0-018].
        A definition body's canonical prefix reaches column 2. In

            :: t
            : ```
            body
            ```

        `body` misses that prefix. S1 stops at the definition entry; S2 does
        not apply and S4 finds no open paragraph. The body closes with an empty
        code block, and `body` and the trailing run are reclassified at document
        level, as for the list-item form (CARVE-P0-013).

     S5 OPENERS. When S3/S4 classify the residue as a container opener (a
        quote marker; a list marker at an admissible column; a `+`
        continuation marker at the marker column, PART 9 §17 L3/L4; a fence
        opener whose closer-lookahead guard holds), push the container and
        recurse on the remaining residue.
   OUTPUT. The layout-resolved line stream (equivalently: INDENT / DEDENT /
           CONT events plus line residues). PARTS 1-2 parse blocks over it;
           inline parsing (PART 3) runs per finished block -- the two-phase
           model of PART 8.
   ============================================================================ *)
